#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124357 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124548 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f00311f-e61b-5845-b19b-59cfbdb5871a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/id #uuid "1a085da3-d8eb-52eb-94ea-4969fb74a83f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5876" :question/problem "What is the domain of $f(x) = \\sqrt{3x+5}-2$?" :question/worked-solution "The domain of\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx \\ge 0\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\sqrt{3x+5} -2\n$$\n\nwe replace $x$ with $3x+5$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n{3x+5} \\ge 0\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x + 5 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge - 5 \\\\ x & \\ge - \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{5}{3}, \\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6f94991ce01ad98ec5bdffbb0673182dc40bc264803b6eea5154241985359c0b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124358 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124549 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc785eb1-cb7e-5368-a092-51f87b7f7c35" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bf9e3a92-47fa-5969-8d05-2d28114f3036" :question/math-academy-id "e-5877" :question/problem "Which of the following values are possible outputs of $f(x) = 2 \\sqrt{x- 2} + 2$?\n\n1. $1$\n2. $2$\n3. $6$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\sqrt x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt x \\ge 0\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts do not affect the range. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x-2} \\ge 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$ and then adding $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{x - 2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x - 2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x - 2} + 2 & \\ge 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in [2,\\infty)$.\n\nAmong the given values, only\n\n$$\nf(x)=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(x)=6\n$$\n\nlie in the range. So, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10d9a611e2fbf8ebf6ae3c12b57e51ea100bf13663586379c42293d4ee45d183.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124359 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124550 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5e23379-0f5d-5691-b10f-5125325ffa70" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformed Square Root Function Given Some Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "cdb0a166-a880-5efb-a747-ac8f940ff50b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5878" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-x}+c$. Now consider the following properties of $f(x)$:\n\n- The domain of $f(x)$ is $x \\in (-\\infty, 2]$.\n- The range is $f(x) \\in [2, \\infty)$.\n- The point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$.\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c$." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-x}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}b - x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\le b\\end{bmatrix}$\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in \\left(-\\infty, 2\\right]$, which is equivalent to $x\\le 2$. Hence, we obtain\n$b=2$.\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0$. Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $2$, we must have\n$a\\sqrt{2 - x}_(⏟)_(\\ge 0) + c \\ge 2$.\nThis means that $c=2$.\n- Finally, since the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have\n$f(1)|= a\\sqrt{2 - 1} + 2; 4|= a + 2; a|= 2$.\n\nTherefore, our function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=2\\sqrt{2-x}+2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\na+b+c = 2 + 2 + 2 = 6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124389 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124581 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58b93ad7-d548-58cb-9cc5-0bcd79e28e4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/id #uuid "fa7bd088-d496-5862-80a0-cd20083b2754" :question/math-academy-id "e-7944" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x) = \\sqrt{6x+7} - 5$." :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6x+7} - 5 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{6x + 7} - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{6x + 7} &= 5 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{6x + 7})^{2} &= 5^{2} \\\\\n6x + 7 &= 25 \\\\\n6x &= 18 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{6 \\cdot 3 + 7} - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{25} &= 5 \\\\\n5 &= 5✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=3$."}]}
